indicators.impact:
Republic Act No. 11127, known as the National Payment Systems Act (NPSA), mandates that all Operators of Payment Systems (OPS) register with the Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas (BSP) within six (6) months from the effectivity of the Act, as stipulated in Section 10. This requirement is further detailed in BSP Circular No. 1049, Series of 2019, which provides the implementing rules and regulations for the registration process.

Under Section 101 of the Manual of Regulations for Payment Systems (MORPS), as introduced by BSP Circular No. 1049, an OPS is defined as any person or entity that performs any of the following functions:
1. Maintains the platform that enables payments or fund transfers, regardless of whether the source and destination accounts are maintained with the same or different institutions;
2. Operates the system or network that enables payments or fund transfers to be made through the use of a payment instrument;
3. Provides a system that processes payments on behalf of any person or the government; and
4. Engages in other activities similar to the foregoing, as may be determined by the Monetary Board.

Entities classified as OPS are required to submit an application for registration to the BSP, which includes:
- A business plan detailing their existing business, business model, and target markets;
- A copy of their business registration or permit indicating their line of business, issued by the city or municipality with territorial jurisdiction over their principal place of business.

Banks and Electronic Money Issuers (EMIs) that perform OPS functions are also required to notify the BSP of their OPS activities. Failure to comply with the registration process may result in penalties, including fines and other enforcement actions, as provided under the NPSA and BSP regulations.

